      "content": "The \"exaggeration\" or \"stoic\" stereotypes often stem from historical biases reflecting the over-policing and medicalization of Black and Brown communities rather than neurobiological differences. These misconceptions, rooted in 19th and early 20th-century studies that conflated race with physiology, frequently lead providers to under-treat minority patients by misinterpreting their atypical pain reports. Understanding that these perceptions are based on flawed data and systemic racism is crucial for avoiding under-diagnosis and ensuring equitable care.",
